1077→    \centering
1078→    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{bottleneck_comparison.pdf}
1079→    \caption{Bottleneck system: flux-based adaptive FSP (solid blue) vs.\ Krylov-FSP-SSA (dashed red). (a) Mean $\langle C \rangle$ trajectory: the adaptive FSP tracks the reference growth of species $C$, while Krylov-FSP-SSA underestimates production after connectivity is lost. (b) Mean $\langle B \rangle$ trajectory. (c) State space size: the adaptive FSP maintains a larger active set to preserve the bottleneck pathway. (d) Krylov-FSP-SSA diagnostics showing step size $\tau$ and probability mass over iterations.}
1080→    \label{fig:bottleneck_comparison}
1081→\end{figure}
1082→
1083→\paragraph{Robertson.}
1084→Table~\ref{tab:comparison_robertson} and Figure~\ref{fig:robertson_comparison} report the Robertson system comparison over $[0, 10]$. Here both methods complete the simulation, but with markedly different computational costs. The flux-based adaptive FSP requires 877\,s due to the small time steps ($\Delta t \sim 1.3 \times 10^{-5}$) needed to track the stiff dynamics at the flux-adaptive scale. The Krylov-FSP-SSA method completes in 0.35\,s using 101 iterations with large accepted steps, because the matrix exponential integrator can take steps exceeding the fastest reaction timescale without losing mass conservation. This result is expected: on a short time horizon without bottleneck states, the accept/reject time stepping of Krylov-FSP-SSA is competitive, and the flux-adaptive rule pays a cost for resolving transient dynamics at finer temporal resolution. Figure~\ref{fig:robertson_comparison}(a) shows the initial $\tau$ halving cascade---from $\tau_{\mathrm{init}} = 0.1$ down to $\sim 10^{-14}$---followed by rapid recovery once the matrix exponential absorbs the fast transient. Panel~(d) confirms that both methods produce consistent mean trajectories.
1085→
1086→However, extending the Robertson system to longer horizons ($t_f = 10^4$) reveals the limitations of the accept/reject approach: starting from $\tau_{\mathrm{init}} = 0.1$, repeated halving is required to satisfy the mass conservation criterion (because the total propensity $\sim 3 \times 10^6$ demands $\tau \ll 10^{-6}$ for stability), and the method makes negligible progress toward $t_f$. Meanwhile, the flux-adaptive method automatically adjusts $\Delta t$ across six orders of magnitude as the system transitions between fast transients and slow equilibration.
1087→
